@startuml 07-variable-resolution
!pragma layout smetana
!pragma useVerticalIf on
' 变量名字、作用域与访问描述符的唯一解析顺序。
' 来源：GameData/ast/variable_table.h, expression_evaluator.h,
'       GameData/Variable/variable_storage.h, GameProc/script_runner.h

title GameData：变量解析

start
:ScriptRunner::stepOnce() supplies explicit ScopeId;
:ExpressionEvaluator receives VariableNode or a runtime name;

if (VariableNode has a valid access key?) then (yes)
  :read/write the keyed locus;
  :use accessKind and VariableKey;
else (no)
  :VariableStorage::resolveIntLocus(name, indices, scope);
  :resolve once to IntLocus;
  :dispatch by locus kind;
endif

:Return value or commit write;
stop

legend right
  The source of scope identity is the script segment
  (script name + segment start), not the function name.
  The loader writes line.scopeKey; a call frame interns it once.

  The resolver is the only ordered name decision:
  parameter -> character -> ARG/LOCAL -> ARGS/LOCALS/RESULTS
  -> system slot -> declared multidimensional slot -> 1D slot.

  `readIntByName` and `writeIntByName` consume the same resolved
  locus. Storage shape and address geometry are outside this diagram.

  CSV name resolution has a separate final lookup in ConstantTable:
  core mappings (FLAG, STR -> STRNAME, etc.) win first, then registered
  EE mappings (for example DAY -> DAY.CSV), and only then an EE-style
  user-variable fallback can use an already loaded CSV whose basename is
  the variable name. This fallback is a name-table lookup only; this layer
  does not claim ERD loading or multi-dimensional user-variable support.
  A missing table or missing name returns -1 and normal numeric indices are
  unaffected.
endlegend
@enduml
